// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Decapitated Army
// @category Military and Warfare
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Decapitated_Army
// @domain   Political
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): cut through to the enemy commander and kill him or drive him from the
// field, and the force he led collapses — its resistance ends with his fall. The army was only as
// strong as its one head: cohesion flowed downward from the leader (the coalition was held by him,
// the commands obeyed because of him), so when he falls the structure has nothing left to stand on
// and the host routs, surrenders, or disintegrates. Rides action_dynamics (the leader's fall —
// the Finisher that leaves him [+Defeated]/[+Fallen]) and power_dynamics (the cohesion that flowed
// from the head — coalition and obedience, which the head's removal cannot survive). The pitiless
// economy of the One-Man Army read from the other side: defeat the man and the mass is moot. When
// the collapse is total and permanent it is the Keystone Army.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict
import "trl/modules/action_dynamics.trl"   // Finisher -> the leader [+Defeated]/[+Fallen]
import "trl/modules/power_dynamics.trl"    // the coalition / obedience that flowed from the head

assoc decapitated_army -> [
  power_dynamics  : "cohesion-from-the-head fails in",
  action_dynamics : "the leader's fall in",
  conflict        : "ends with one death a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// Private to this trope: a force whose cohesion was load-bearing on a single head, and the rout
// that follows the head's removal. Kept distinct from power_dynamics' [+Fragmented] — a routed
// host is the specific battlefield collapse (flight / surrender), not merely a sundered coalition.
imply DecapitatedArmy -> [Rout, Collapse, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: a force whose cohesion ran through one commander, that commander now [+Fallen],
// has nothing to hold it — it is left [+Leaderless] and breaks ([+Routed]), and its prior
// cohesion is gone ([-Cohesive]). One death, and the mass is moot.
rule Decapitated_Army {
  when:
    char $force  [+Collective] [+Cohesive]
    char $leader [+Leader] [@Leads(other=$force)]
    $leader [+Fallen]
  then:
    $force [+Leaderless]
    $force [+Routed]
    $force [-Cohesive]
    $force -- collapse
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: the Battle of Issus — Alexander breaks the Persians (history, 333 BC) ===
// Alexander's phalanx pins the vast Persian host while he and the Companion cavalry drive straight
// for Darius' chariot. The line does not have to be beaten — only the king reached. When Darius
// turns and flees the field, the army that had cohered around the Great King's person dissolves
// behind him: the wings rout, the host scatters, and the empire that stood on that one head begins
// to come apart. The phalanx held; the cavalry decapitated; the mass was moot.

// DARIUS III, GREAT KING OF PERSIA
char darius [+Leader] [+Ruler]

// THE PERSIAN HOST
char persians [+Antagonist] [+Collective] [+Cohesive]
darius [@Leads(other=persians)]

// ALEXANDER AND THE COMPANION CAVALRY
char alexander [+Protagonist]

// EXT. THE PINARUS RIVER, ISSUS - DAY
set issus [+Exterior] [+Day] [+Contested]

scene the_break {
  beat 1 {
    alexander @ issus
    darius @ issus
    evt the_charge [&Finishes(agent=alexander, target=darius)]
    // (in the field: Darius does not die but flees — reached, unseated, removed as the head)
    darius [+Fallen]
  }
  beat 2 {
    persians @ issus
    // Decapitated_Army -> persians [+Leaderless] [+Routed] [-Cohesive]
  }
}
